## Thread.Abort → .NET 8 Migration

### Pattern Classification

This is a **timeout enforcement** pattern — a thread is aborted if it exceeds a time limit. It also contains a **Thread.ResetAbort** catch block.

### Why it breaks on .NET 8

- `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006)
- `Thread.ResetAbort()` also throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`
- `ThreadAbortException` is never raised — the catch block is dead code

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(), token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Work doesn't stop automatically** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work()` is a long CPU loop, it will keep running in the background after timeout. Pass `CancellationToken` into the work delegate if possible. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using` — it holds timer resources. |
| **`Task.WaitAsync` ≠ killing the task** | It unblocks the *caller*, but the task itself continues. There is no .NET 8 equivalent to forcibly killing a thread. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at this top-level orchestration point. The `when` guard ensures you only catch *your* timeout, not an unrelated cancellation. |

### For truly uncooperative code

If `work()` cannot be modified to check a token (e.g., third-party library, long native call), the only safe option is to run it in a **separate process** and call `Process.Kill()` on timeout. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET.

### Ideal version (if you control the work)

```csharp
// Pass the token all the way down so work actually stops
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);

    try
    {
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

This way the work itself can call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints, ensuring it actually terminates promptly.
